- in_text_citation: [(Miyazaki et al., 2016-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/700fb459fbe0b33f23713befdb1f424d7c97c524)
- venue: Nature Communications
- citation count: 41
[method]
The paper describes a method for developing chemically controllable protein aggregates using a destabilizing-domain-based construct referred to as AgDD. The approach relies on Shield-1-dependent control of the DD, with Shield-1 withdrawal used to trigger a change in the protein state that leads to aggregate formation. The method also includes quantification of aggregates and bioinformatic analysis to compare the properties of AgDD aggregates with known aggregates. This relates to the proposed research method because both approaches use Shield-1-sensitive destabilizing domains as conditional molecular tools to induce a defined protein stress state, although the paper focuses on aggregate formation rather than compartment-specific unfolded protein stress in the nucleus or cytosol.
[results]
The supplied paper excerpt does not provide numerical values, statistical tests, or quantitative comparisons to baselines. The reported outcome was that AgDD aggregates resemble known aggregates, indicating that Shield-1 withdrawal produced a recognizable aggregate phenotype. No specific aggregate abundance, size distribution, time course, or significance values are available in the provided text.
[context]
The available context is limited to the use of Shield-1 withdrawal, aggregate quantification, and bioinformatic comparison of AgDD aggregates to known aggregates. No cell type, construct details, Shield-1 concentration, withdrawal duration, imaging or quantification instruments, or statistical parameters are provided in the supplied excerpt. This context is relevant to the proposed plan as a precedent for using Shield-1-controlled destabilizing domains, but it does not establish the nuclear or cytosolic targeting, RNA sequencing time points, proteasome reporter design, or p53-dependence described in the proposed research plan.
[end related work 1]




[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Love et al., 2014-11)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/817fc8de25f989b8229847fc7710750f273460b6)
- venue: Genome Biology
- citation count: 51468
[method]
The paper describes a negative-binomial generalized linear model pipeline for RNA-seq differential expression analysis, corresponding to the DESeq2 approach. Inputs are raw integer read counts per gene and a design matrix encoding experimental factors such as condition and time. The method estimates size factors by median-of-ratios normalization, estimates gene-wise and trended dispersions with empirical Bayes shrinkage, and shrinks log2 fold-change estimates using an empirical prior based on Fisher information. Hypothesis testing uses Wald tests or likelihood-ratio tests, independent filtering, Benjamini-Hochberg false discovery rate control, and optional minimum or maximum effect-size thresholds. The paper also describes a regularized log transformation that can be used to stabilize variance before downstream visualization or clustering. This method is directly relevant to the proposed research plan because it can be applied to the RNA-seq time-course data from nuclear, cytosolic, and non-targeted destabilizing-domain stress to identify commonly and distinctly regulated genes, test contrasts between compartments, and generate transformed count matrices suitable for clustering. Pathway enrichment analysis is not part of the paper's method.
[results]
The paper's benchmark sections indicate that the method controlled false discovery rate at the nominal 5% level and outperformed DESeq, edgeR, and Cuffdiff in sensitivity and precision in simulation benchmarks. In RNA-seq benchmarks, it showed lower false-positive rates and higher concordance with microarray-defined differential genes than the compared methods. The supplied excerpt does not include the numeric values, so specific gene counts, p-values, fold changes, sensitivity percentages, or precision percentages cannot be reported.
[context]
The method was applied to RNA-seq count data with biological replicates, including the Bottomly and Pickrell RNA-seq datasets, using design matrices and contrasts for condition comparisons. The benchmark context involved small-to-moderate RNA-seq experiments, FDR thresholding at 5%, and microarray comparisons as external validation. The simulation benchmark used 10,000 genes, 4 conditions, 3 replicates, 10% differential genes, and fold changes between 2 and 10. This is analogous to the proposed 0-240 minute time-course with biological replicates for nuclear, cytosolic, and non-targeted destabilizing-domain constructs, but the paper does not address p53 knockout, proteasome reporters, or pathway enrichment.
[end related work 1]


[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Beer et al., 2019-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b773b974d4ab13b96856205fe70133284f5e0198)
- venue: Nature Communications
- citation count: 23
[method]
The paper used degron-tagged fluorescent reporter constructs to probe ubiquitin/proteasome-mediated degradation and membrane topology. Plasmids were built in a pIRES-hygro3 backbone, and reporter fluorescence was imaged by light microscopy. Fluorescence intensity measurements were used to infer whether degrons were accessible to the degradation machinery, with statistical evaluation applied to the intensity data. The degron protection assay and tracking of phagocytosed cargo were used to test how membrane topology, organelle association, and ubiquitin ligase adaptor localization influence degradation. This is directly related to the proposed method because it uses the same general principle—fluorescent degron reporters whose accumulation or loss reports proteasome/ubiquitin pathway activity—but applies it to membrane-bound compartments and cargo rather than to nuclear versus cytosolic proteasome capacity.
[results]
The supplied excerpt reports qualitative outcomes rather than numerical values. It states that a degron protection assay reveals the topology of the tagged protein; degron reporters enable tracking of phagocytosed cargo; degron reporters reveal membrane topology and dynamics; ubiquitin ligase adaptor localization determines degradation; single membrane organelles protect degrons from degradation; and degron-tagged reporters do not degrade binding partners. No fold changes, fluorescence intensity values, sample sizes, or statistical significance values are provided in the available text.
[context]
Experimental context includes plasmid construction within a pIRES-hygro3 backbone, light microscopy, quantification of corpse membrane breakdown, fluorescence intensity measurements, statistical evaluation, and statements on biological material and data availability. The paper’s assay appears to involve phagocytosed cargo, corpse membrane breakdown, and abscission, suggesting a cell-based degradation reporter system in which membrane topology and organelle localization modulate degron accessibility. Specific cell type, degron sequence, ligand or treatment conditions, exposure times, microscope settings, and statistical tests are not specified in the provided excerpt. For the proposed research plan, the relevant transferable context is that degron reporter fluorescence can be used to compare degradation in different compartments, but the paper does not itself test nuclear versus cytosolic proteasome capacity or Shield-1-dependent destabilizing domains.
[end related work 1]

