- in_text_citation: [(Kirschner et al., 2015-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5e7d1dbb4f2647152acdc21f93a616ce04e8cfb5)
- venue: PLoS Genetics
- citation count: 63
[method]
The paper used a p53-inducible H1299 cell line, a p53-deficient human lung carcinoma background, to test p53-dependent transcriptional outputs. After acute and chronic p53 induction, global gene expression was profiled by microarray, and p53 target engagement was examined with ChIP-seq, quantitative RT-PCR, motif analysis, and recursive partitioning approaches. This relates to the proposed CRISPR p53 knockout because both strategies use a p53-deficient or p53-controllable cellular background to determine whether observed transcriptional changes require p53, although the paper uses an inducible p53-null system rather than CRISPR knockout and does not use destabilizing-domain unfolded protein stress or nuclear versus cytosolic compartmentalization.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical fold changes, p-values, or array/sequencing metrics. The reported outcomes are qualitative: p53-responsive gene expression was phenotype-specific, acute and chronic p53 conditions produced distinct genomic binding profiles, chronic p53 formed extensive self-regulatory hubs, and p53 repressed the lipogenic enzyme SCD in radiation-induced senescence, p53 apoptosis, and cancer contexts.
[context]
The experiments were performed in H1299 cells, a p53-deficient human lung carcinoma line, with inducible p53 expression used to generate acute and chronic p53 conditions. The paper lists microarray, ChIP-seq, quantitative RT-PCR, luciferase, immunoblotting, proliferation, SA-β-galactosidase, and viability assays, but the provided text does not specify the induction reagent, concentration, time course, array platform, sequencing depth, or statistical thresholds. The proposed plan instead uses mouse NIH3T3 fibroblasts, Shield-1-dependent destabilizing-domain GFP fusions, RNA sequencing at 0-240 minutes, and CRISPR p53 knockout, so the paper provides a conceptual precedent for testing p53 dependence rather than a direct methodological match.
[end related work 1]

