[method]
The paper used CRISPR/Cas9-mediated TP53 knockout in H1 human pluripotent stem cells to test whether p53 is required for the Cas9 double-strand break response. LentiCRISPR sgRNAs targeting TP53 were introduced into H1-iCas9 cells, clones were isolated, and editing was verified by CRISPR indel analysis from genomic DNA and protein-level assays. Cas9 double-strand breaks were induced by doxycycline in wild-type and TP53 knockout cells, and the transcriptional response was measured by RNA-seq and qPCR, with viability assessed by live imaging of confluency and FACS. This is directly analogous to the proposed CRISPR p53 knockout to test p53 requirement in compartment-specific unfolded protein transcriptional responses, except the stress is Cas9-induced double-strand breaks rather than nuclear destabilizing-domain unfolding.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical fold changes, differentially expressed gene counts, or p-values. The reported p53-dependent toxicity section indicates that TP53 knockout reduced or abolished the Cas9 double-strand break-induced transcriptional program and decreased Cas9-induced cell death relative to wild-type H1-iCas9 cells. p53 target genes were no longer induced in TP53 knockout cells, while non-p53 double-strand break response genes were less affected. No statistical significance values are available from the provided text.
[context]
Experiments were performed in H1 human pluripotent stem cells and H1-iCas9 doxycycline-inducible Cas9 lines, with parental H1 and H1-iCas9 cells without doxycycline as controls. CRISPR indel analysis from genomic DNA, RNA-seq and qPCR, live imaging of confluency, FACS, and immunofluorescence/microscopy were used. The context is Cas9-induced double-strand breaks in human pluripotent stem cells, not nuclear unfolded protein stress, but the p53 knockout logic is directly relevant to the proposed research plan.
